1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The idea of the Scotch is to immediately contest the centre and open up the diagonal for the bishop. } 3... exd4 { Respond to the tension on the centre. } 4. Nxd4 Bc5 { Develop with tempo } 5. Nxc6 Qf6! { Threatened mate in 1 } 6. f3? { Stop mate in 1 threat but weakened king side: white can't castle king side due to bc5. } (6. Qf3 { Correct response for white. (Goes into end game) } 6... Qxf3 7. gxf3 dxc6) 6... dxc6 { Development is more important than small deficiency in the pawn structure like double pawns. } (6... Qxc6 7. Nc3?? d6 8. Bb5 { [%cal Gb5e8] }) 7. Bc4 Ne7 8. Bf4?? { Perhaps tunnel vision on the undefended c7 pawn. } (8. Qe2 { Prepare to develop bishop to e3 to castle king side. Also possible to develop all the pieces to castle queen side. } 8... O-O 9. Nc3 Bd7 10. Be3 Rad8 11. O-O) 8... Qxf4 9. Qd2 Qe5 10. f4 { [%csl Ge4,Gb2][%cal Ge5e4,Ge5b2] } 10... Qxb2 11. f5 Qxa1 12. Ke2 Qe5 { Avoid getting trapped.
White resign } *


