1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 Bc5 (5... Nxd4? 6. Qxd4 c5 7. Qd1 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] }) (5... Bb4 6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3) 6. Be3 Bb6 { Defend bishop to prevent tactical blunder. (Check sideline) } (6... O-O?? 7. Nxc6 dxc6 (7... Bxe3 8. Nxd8) 8. Bxc5) 7. Qf3?? (7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. Bxb6 axb6 { [%cal Ga8a1] } 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O Nxe4 (10... b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Ga8a2,Gc3b1] }) 11. Nxe4 Rxa2) 7... Nxd4 8. Bxd4 Bxd4 9. Qg3 O-O 10. O-O-O Bxc3 11. Qxc3 Nxe4 12. Qd4 d5! { Protect the knight and open up the diagonal to develop the bishop. } 13. Bd3 (13. Qxd5 { Note: Whenever there's a x-ray between a rook and a queen and there is a bunch of pieces in the way, you have to pay close attention to the x-ray because x-ray tactics are amongst the most easily and most often missed tactics on the chessboard. } 13... Qxd5 14. Rxd5 { [%cal Ge4f2] }) (13. Qxe4?? { [%cal Gd1d8] } 13... Qg5+ (13... dxe4 14. Rxd8 Rxd8 { [%csl Gd8,Ga8,Gh1] }) 14. f4 (14. Qe3 { [%cal Gg5e3] })) 13... Bf5 14. Qe5 (14. f3 Nd6 { [%cal Gd6f5] }) 14... Bg6 15. Rhe1 Qg5+ { White pieces are more active than black; trading queens will benefit black. } 16. Qxg5 Nxg5 17. Bb5 c6 18. Bxc6 bxc6 19. Rd4 Rfe8 { Black is up in materials trading pieces gives black more advantage and helps to simplify the game further. } 20. Rxe8+ Rxe8 21. b3 h6 { Avoid any chances for back-rank mate. } 22. f3 Re2 23. Kb2 Rxc2+ 24. Ka3 Ne6 { Removing the rook (Blockader) from d file to potentially promote the past pawn or trade white's rook for a past pawn. } 25. Rb4 d4 26. Rb8+ Kh7 27. h4 d3 28. Rb4 { [%cal Gb4d4,Ge6d4] } 28... d2 29. g4 d1=Q 30. g5 Qc1+ 31. Ka4 Rxa2# *


