[UTCTime "16:57:47"]

1. g3 e5 2. Bg2 d5 3. e3 Nf6 4. d4 { Allow black to decide to have an open or close game. } 4... e4 { Closing the centre } 5. c4! { Attacking base of the pawn chain } 5... c6 6. Qc2 $15 { Aimless move } 6... Bd6 { Continue developing } 7. h4? { Weakened the g4 square } { [%csl Gg4] } 7... O-O 8. a3 Bg4 { Taking advantage of the weakness created by white pushing pawn to h4 } 9. b4 dxc4! { *Key moment
White is so undeveloped that black can afford to sacrifice the integrity of the pawn chain and open up the centre.
If black allows white to close the centre with c5, white can just comfortably develop his pieces. } 10. Bxe4?! { Bad mistake - White is giving up his only piece that can defend all of the weak light squares around his king } 10... Nxe4 11. Qxe4 f5 { Defending with tempo } 12. Qe6+ Kh8 13. Qxc4 a5 { Continue with the plan of opening up the position to infiltrate white's king that is still in the centre and also not giving white any chances to develop his pieces.
The more black opens up, the harder it is for white to protect their pieces and their weak pawns. } 14. b5 cxb5 15. Qxb5 Qc7 { Occupy the open c file } { [%cal Gc7c1,Gc7g3,Gc7b7] } 16. Nd2 Bxg3 { Again white is so undeveloped that sacrificing a piece to open up the position to white's king is a small price to pay. } 17. fxg3 Qxg3+ 18. Kf1 f4 { Plan to open up the f file for a discover attack from the rook } { [%cal Gf8f1] } 19. Ndf3 (19. e4 f3 { If white decide to not open up the f file, simply just advance the pawn to participate in the attack. } 20. Ngxf3 { If white takes, black just essentially open up the f file. } 20... Bxf3) 19... Bxf3 20. Nxf3 Qxf3+ 21. Ke1 Qxh1+ 22. Kd2 fxe3+ 23. Kxe3 Nc6 { Bringing the rest of the pieces into the game to make it easier for black to deliver a checkmate rather than hunting for a mate with very few pieces } 24. Bb2 Rae8+ 25. Kd3 Qf3+ 26. Kc4 Qe2+ { Whenever the king and queen is in the same diagonal always look for tactics to win the queen. } 27. Kc5 Rf5+ 28. d5 Rxd5+ { Distracting the king from defending the queen.
(Note: The two worse defenders of a piece are the king and the queen. When the king or the queen defends a piece, that piece is generally vulnerable - The moment the queen is attack it has to move and abandon the piece it's defending.) } { [%cal Gc5b5,Gc5d5] } 29. Kxd5 Qxb5+ 30. Kd6 Qxb2 31. Rd1 Qe5+ 32. Kd7 Qe7# *


