1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bf4 O-O 6. e3 c5 { Create tension in the centre } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. cxd5 exd5 { (Note: The lesser the pieces there are the weaker the isolated queens pawn. The reason is that there are not enough pieces to defend the pawn.)
Often times the battle of the isolated queen's pawn (IQB) is occurring on the square that is in front of it. Is the side that is playing against the IQB going to be able to establish a blockade on that square or is the side that has the IQB going to be able to push the IQB forward, absolve themselves of the IQB and as a consequence, develop with a tremendous initiative. } 9. Bg5 { Pinning the defender of d5 pawn } { [%cal Gg5d8,Gf6d5] } (9. Be2 { One of the possible way to utilize the isolated queen's pawn is to prepare a pawn push } 9... Nc6 10. O-O d4) 9... Be6 { Adding a defender to the d5 pawn as the knight on f3 no longer defends it due to the pin } 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 11. Nxd5? Qxb2 12. Nf4?? Bb4+ 13. Nd2 { White's queen is overloaded, this move allows black to move white's queen from defending the rook } { [%cal Gd1a1,Gd1d2,Gb4d2,Gb2a1] } 13... Bxd2+ 14. Qxd2 Qxa1+ 15. Qd1 Qxd1+ 16. Kxd1 Bxa2 17. Bd3 Nc6 18. Ke2 a5 { Identify and advance the past pawn } { [%cal Ga5a4,Ga4a3,Ga3a2,Ga2a1] } 19. Ra1 Bb3 20. Rb1 a4 21. Bb5 a3 22. Bxc6 bxc6 23. Kd3 a2 24. Ra1 Rfd8+ 25. Kc3 Rd1 { White resign } *


