1. e4 e5 2. f4 { White is weakening his king side } 2... exf4 3. Nf3 d5 { (Note: By principles when your opponent has weakened their kingside you generally want to start opening up the centre in order to be able to exploit that.) } 4. exd5 Nf6 5. d4 Nxd5 6. Bc4 Be7 { Continue developing naturally } 7. O-O O-O 8. Ne5 (8. Bxd5 Qxd5 9. Bxf4 c6) 8... Be6 9. Bd3 Nc6 { Contest control of the centre } { [%cal Gc6d4,Gc6e5] } 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Qf3 c5 12. Bxf4 Nxf4 13. Qxf4 Qxd4+ { Sacrificing the queen to remedy/correct the pawn structure. } 14. Qxd4 (14. Kh1 Qxb2 15. Nd2 Qd4) 14... cxd4 15. Nd2 Rab8 16. b3 Bg5 { Preparing to move to the e3 outpost where the bishop can be most active } { [%csl Ge3] } 17. Nf3 Be3+ 18. Kh1 f6 { Stop white's knight from centralising on e5 (Active square) } { [%cal Gf3e5] } 19. Nh4 { Prepare to centralise on f5 } { [%cal Gh4f5] } 19... g6 { Again preventing white's knight from moving to an active square in this case the f4 square } { [%cal Gg6f5,Gh4f5] } 20. a4 { [%cal Ga4a5] } 20... a5 { Preventing white's pawn from advancing to a5 } 21. Rf3 Bd5 { Centralising and improving the position of the bishop } 22. Rff1 f5 { Prepare to support the dark square bishop on e3 } { [%cal Gf5f4,Gf4e3] } 23. Nf3 { Again preparing to centralise the knight of e5 } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] } 23... Rfe8 { Preventing white's knight from centralising } 24. h3 c5 25. Nh2 Kg7 { Improving the king } 26. Rfe1 Rbc8 { Preparing to support c4 pawn break } { [%cal Gc5c4] } 27. Bb5 Re4 { Potentially get onto the 2nd rank when possible } { [%cal Ge4f4,Gf4f2] } 28. Rac1?? Bxc1 29. Rxc1 c4 { The idea of this pawn break is to open the position up as all black's pieces are positionally thus allowing black to dominate more squares. } 30. bxc4 Bxc4 31. Nf3 Bxb5 32. axb5 d3 33. c4 Rexc4 { Offering the trade so that black can promote his past pawn } 34. Rb1 Rc1+ 35. Rxc1 Rxc1+ { White resign } *


