1. e4 e5 (1... d5) 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6 4. c3 h6 $14 5. d4 Bg4? { [%csl Gb7,Gf7] } 6. Qb3 { Exploiting the fact that the b7 and f7 pawn are weak } { [%csl Gb7,Gf7][%cal Gc8g4,Gc8b7] } 6... Qd7 7. Qxb7 (7. Bxf7+ { A better forcing variation } 7... Qxf7 8. Qxb7 { [%cal Gb7a8,Gb7c6] }) 7... Rb8 8. Qa6 Bxf3 9. gxf3 exd4 10. Bb5 { Pinning to remove the defender of the d5 square so that white can recapture and gain control of the centre } { [%cal Gc6d4,Gb5d7] } 10... Rb6 11. Qa4 { Reinforce the pressure on the diagonal } 11... Nge7 12. cxd4 { Black's knight can't recapture the pawn due to the pin } 12... d5 13. Nc3 { Keep the tension in the centre and continue to develop } 13... g6 14. Be3 Bg7 15. O-O-O O-O 16. h4 { White is starting a pawn storm due to opposite side castling to try to tear open black's kingside defence. (Maybe unsound) } 16... Qd6 17. h5 Ra8 { Black prepare to start his own attack on the queenside. Unfortunately it's slower than white's attack. } { [%cal Ga7a6] } 18. hxg6 fxg6 19. e5 { Remove any potential threat of black forking white's king and a piece with check } { [%cal Gd6f4] } (19. Bxh6 Bxh6+ 20. Rxh6 Qf4+) 19... Qe6 20. Bxh6 a6 { This is not a threat to white because the pawn is pinned } { [%cal Ga4a8,Ga6b5] } 21. Bxg7 Kxg7 22. Bxc6 { Capturing black piece to absolve the role of white's queen being a defending piece so that white can bring his queen to participate in an attacking role on the kingside. } { [%cal Ga4c2,Gc2d2,Gd2h6] } 22... Qxc6 23. Qc2 { [%cal Gc2d2,Gd2h6] } 23... Rab8 24. b3 { Taking care of the b-file pressure } { [%cal Gb8b3] } 24... Qe6 25. Na4 { [%cal Ga4c5] } 25... Rb5 26. Nc5 Qc6 27. Rh3 { [%cal Gd1h1] } 27... a5 28. Rdh1 { Black resign } *


