1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nd4 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. O-O c6 6. Bc4 b5 7. Bb3 Nf6? 8. e5 Nd5?! 9. Bxd5 cxd5 $16 { [%csl Gd5,Gd4,Gd7] } 10. b3 { Prepare to fianchetto the bishop to attack the d4 pawn } { [%cal Gc1b2,Gb2d4] } 10... Bb7 11. Bb2 Bc8 12. Bxd4 Be7 13. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3b5,Gc3d5] } 13... Bb7 14. Qg4 { Develop the queen to potentially accumulate the chances on attacking black's kingside } 14... O-O 15. f4 { Prepare either a rook lift to bring more pieces to execute an attack or just a pawn push to f6 to potentially execute a lobster pincer mate on g7. } { [%cal Gf1f3,Gf3g3,Gf4f5,Gf5f6,Gg4g7] } 15... a6 16. f5 f6 17. Rae1 { Again bringing more pieces to execute an attack } 17... Qc7 18. exf6 Bxf6 19. Re7!! { It would checkmate in 1 if black captures white's rook } { [%cal Gf6e7,Gg4g7,Gd4g7] } 19... Bxd4+ 20. Qxd4 { Repositioning to threaten checkmate } { [%cal Gd4g7,Ge7g7] } 20... Rf6 21. Nxd5 { [%cal Gd5c7,Gd5f6] } 21... Bxd5 22. Qxd5+ { [%cal Gd5g8,Gd5a8] } 22... Kf8 23. Qxa8+ Kxe7 24. Re1+ { Starting a king hunt to bring the black's king towards the centre where there are less pieces to shield the king. } 24... Kd6 25. Qxa6+ Kc5 26. b4+ Kd5 27. Qxb5+ Kd6 28. c4 { [%cal Gb5d5] } 28... Qc6 29. Qe5# *


