1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Ba3 { To demonstrate playing with a piece down } 3. bxa3 Nc6 4. Bb2 d6 5. d4 exd4 6. Nxd4 Nf6 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. Qf3 Qe7 { Using the knowledge that white wants trade the bishop for black's knight to potentially get onto e5 utilizing the diagonal that white has given up by trading.
Also pinning white's e4 pawn to the king to supervise a pawn push towards the centre knowing that white's pawn cannot capture it. } { [%cal Gb2f6,Ge7e1,Gd6d5] } 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Bc4 d5 { To try accumulate pressure on white's central pawn while white's king is in the centre } 11. Bb3 f5 { Prepare to capture with f-pawn to establish a pawn chain which allows helps to lock up the centre. As long as black has central control with the pawn chain, it is difficult for white to exploit the fact that black's king is still in the centre. } 12. O-O fxe4 { [%cal Gd5e4,Gc6d5] } 13. Qe3 Rg8 { x-raying } { [%cal Gg8g1] } 14. Kh1 f5 { Improving the pawn chain } 15. Nc3 Be6 16. Rae1 Qg7 { Accumulating pressure on the g2 pawn } { [%cal Gg7g2] } 17. Qg3 (17. g3 d4 18. Qe2 Bxb3 (18... dxc3 19. Bxe6) 19. axb3 dxc3) 17... Qf6 { Keeping the tension } { [%cal Gf6c3,Gg8g3] } 18. Qh3 O-O-O { Castling to remove the threat of white's rook x-ray to later prepare a pawn push without putting black's king at risk of a cross-fire } { [%cal Gf5f4,Gc3e4,Gd5e4,Ge1e8,Ge6h3] } (18... f4 { [%cal Ge6h3,Ge1e8] } 19. Nxe4 { [%cal Ge4f6] } 19... Bxh3 (19... dxe4 20. Qxe6+ (20. Rxe4 { [%cal Gh3e6,Ge4e6,Gb3e6] }) 20... Qxe6 21. Rxe4 { [%cal Ge4e8] }) 20. Nxf6+ { [%cal Ge1e8,Gf6e8,Gf6g8] }) 19. Ne2 f4 20. Qc3 f3!! { Botez Gambit to set up a mating trap } { [%cal Gc3f6] } 21. Qxf6 (21. g3 fxe2 (21... Qxc3 22. Nxc3) (21... Qh6 { [%cal Gf3g2,Gh6h3,Ge2f4,Gd8f8] } 22. Nf4 { [%cal Gf4g2] } 22... Bh3 23. a4 Qxf4 24. gxf4 Bg2+ 25. Kg1 Bh3+ 26. Kh1 Bg2+ 27. Kg1 Bh3+ 28. Kh1 { Perpetual }) 22. Qxf6) 21... fxg2+ 22. Kg1 gxf1=Q+ 23. Kxf1 Bh3# *


