1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Ne2 { White has a plan to undermine black's central pawn chain by pushing the f-pawn so developing the knight to e2 square allows white to push his f-pawn without blockading it } { [%cal Gf2f4,Gf4f5,Gf5e6] } 6... c5 7. c3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nc6 9. Nbc3 Nge7 10. O-O Nf5 11. Be3 { Adding a defender to the d4 pawn first with the idea of relocating white's knight to the g3 square offering a trade to remove black's knight from occupying the f5 square so that white can push the f-pawn } { [%cal Ge3d4,Ge2g3,Gg3f5,Gf2f4,Gf4f5] } 11... Bb4 12. Ng3 Nxe3 13. fxe3 { Change of plan because it's stronger to open up the f-file for white's rook than pushing the pawn. Capturing also helps to defend the central pawn with a pawn rather than a piece. } { [%cal Gf1f8,Ge3d4] } 13... O-O 14. Rf4 { Doubling the rook } { [%cal Ga1f1] } 14... Bxc3 15. bxc3 Ne7 16. Raf1 Ng6 17. Rg4 { [%cal Gg4g8] } 17... Rc8 18. h4 { White is sacrificing pawn to buy himself some time to reposition his knight for a rook lift to the h-file to execute a strong attack. Since black already has a plan of his own to capture white's undefended c3 pawn. (Unsound) } { [%cal Gd8c7,Gc7c3,Gg3h5,Gf1f3,Gf3h3,Gg6h4] } 18... Nxh4 19. Nh5 Ng6 { These two moves it took black allowed white to } 20. Rf3 Kh8 21. Rh3 { [%cal Gh3h8] } 21... Rg8 22. Rxg6 { Sacrificing with the idea of mating black's king } 22... fxg6 23. Qxg6 h6 (23... Rc7 24. Qxh7+ Kxh7 25. Nf4+ { [%csl Gg6,Gh5] } 25... Qh4 26. Rxh4#) (23... hxg6 24. Nf6#) 24. Nf6 gxf6 25. Rxh6# *


